  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there is: Two-dimensional solids are distinguished from bulk solids by constrained longitudinal dimensionality."];
  "N11" [label="(5,0,0) Currently there is: Some types of 2D metal oxide nanosheets have been produced via the “top-down” strategy, similar to graphene exfoliation from layered graphite."];
  "N12" [label="(6,0,0) Currently there is: Nine types of 2D hexagonal metal oxide monolayers (TiO₂, MnO, Fe₂O₃ etc.) have been prepared via exfoliation method."];
  "N13" [label="(7,0,0) Currently there is: The bulk metal surface could be polished and oxidized to layered metal oxide in oxygen-deficient environment, easily obtained after mechanical exfoliation."];
  "N14" [label="(8,0,0) Currently there is: Liquid-phase exfoliation also was used in metal oxide sheets production."];
  "N15" [label="(9,0,0) Currently there is: Ultrasonic waves could break the van der Waals forces between the metal oxide layers, following the liquid-phase exfoliation and stabilization in liquids."];
  "N16" [label="(10,0,0) If the original frameworks contain a layered structure relying on weak vdW forces to maintain the interlayer interaction, then exfoliation is suitable for cleavage."];
  "N17" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given specific cases of top-down exfoliation methods and examples for layered MOs, and if layered with vdW then suitable, therefore top-down exfoliation is a viable strategy for some layered 2D metal oxides."];
  "N18" [label="(11,0,0) Currently there is: Most metal oxides are not intrinsically layered."];
  "N19" [label="(0,0,0) If the original frameworks do not contain a layered structure relying on weak vdW forces, then the formation of 2D metal oxide nanosheets via exfoliation is quite limited."];
